 ## Abstract
-We describe a deterministic tactile reflex controller for a robotic gripper and evaluate it in simulation only. The design (FPGA RTL, AD7476A-class SPI ADC input, PWM output intended for a DRV8871 H-bridge) enforces a force limit with hysteresis, a slope-based predictive pre-limit, a latched e-stop, and sensor-fault handling that forces the drive inhibited. We run 15 scripted scenarios (159 checks) through (a) a small software model of the documented behaviour and (b) the real RTL under Icarus Verilog on identical stimulus, and compare both with a threshold-only baseline. The model met 159/159 checks and the RTL 157/159; the two RTL misses are a stimulus-granularity artefact, not a controller fault (Section 6.3). The baseline met 93/159 by design. On a ramp of ~277 counts per 3 ms window the predictive pre-limit tripped 12 sample slots (6 ms of simulated time) before the hard limit in both model and RTL. RTL force-to-inhibit latency was 100–189 clock cycles (2.0–3.8 µs at 50 MHz), dominated by waiting for the next ADC frame. No physical hardware was used; no real-world safety claim is made.
+We describe a deterministic tactile reflex controller for a robotic gripper and evaluate it in simulation only. The design (FPGA RTL, AD7476A-class SPI ADC input, PWM output intended for a DRV8871 H-bridge) enforces a force limit with hysteresis, a slope-based predictive pre-limit, a latched e-stop, and sensor-fault handling that forces the drive inhibited. We run 15 scripted scenarios (159 checks) through (a) a small software model of the documented behaviour and (b) the real RTL under Icarus Verilog on identical stimulus, and compare both with a threshold-only baseline. The model met 159/159 checks and the RTL 157/159; the two RTL misses arise because the step-level stimulus for S06a, intended as one invalid sample, lasted about 240 ADC frames in the RTL (Section 6.3). The baseline met 93/159 by design. A separate frame-granular comparison (577 frames, 9 patterns) and an expansion of every step into 240 model samples (839 steps) showed no model–RTL differences; a dropout-timing difference between the model's watchdog and the RTL's is reported as an open limitation (Section 6.4). On a ramp of ~277 counts per 3 ms window the predictive pre-limit tripped 12 stimulus steps (6 ms of stimulus time) before the hard limit in both model and RTL. In single trials the RTL asserted `safe_state` 101–189 clock cycles (2.0–3.8 µs at 50 MHz) after a force-limit stimulus and 100 cycles after an invalid ADC value; these are single-trial values at different ADC frame phases, not bounds. No physical hardware was used; no real-world safety claim is made.
 
 **Keywords:** tactile sensing, force limiting, reflex control, FPGA, fault handling, simulation-based verification
 
 ## 1. Introduction and research question
-Grippers must stop driving when contact force exceeds a limit even if higher-level software is slow or wrong. Question: *in simulation, does a hardware reflex path with hysteresis, slope prediction, a latched e-stop and sensor-fault inhibit behave as specified at, just below and just above its thresholds, and how does it differ from a threshold-only controller on identical inputs?* We make no novelty claim; a literature search for prior art was not performed **[TODO]**.
